meta {
  name: Ask a grounded question
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/chat/sessions/{{chatSessionId}}/messages
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

body:json {
  {
    "content": "What is photosynthesis?"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

tests {
  test("streams citations, then tokens, then done", function () {
    const body = res.getBody().toString();
    expect(body).to.contain("event: citations");
    expect(body).to.contain("event: done");
  });
}

docs {
  Server-sent events, not JSON: `citations` first so the UI can render the sources it is
  about to see cited, then `token` repeatedly, then `done`.

  Bruno shows the raw stream rather than rendering it — that is fine, and it is the
  easiest way to see the frame order.

  Ask something the book does not cover and you get the grounded refusal instead, with
  an empty citations array and **no LLM call at all**. There is nothing to ground an
  answer in, so there is nothing to generate.

  Retrieval is capped at `RETRIEVAL_MAX_DISTANCE` (0.35 cosine). Worth knowing: a small
  document becomes one large chunk whose vector is an average of everything in it, so a
  narrow question can land just over the line while a broad one sails under.
}
